i~B!’ÍΩ1∑º}‡\Ωå˚ˆ&iÍiT•/ÄËÁê#€{"url":"/gcsl/language/context/","fragment":{"page_number":0,"data":{"url":"/gcsl/language/context/","content":"Context. Some values matter deep inside a program without every function in between caring about them: the platform you are building for, for example, or the toolchains available. A context is a named value of that kind. You declare it once, read it with ^, and set it for a call and everything that call does with with: Copycontext arch: String = \"x86_64\" fn compiler(): String { \"gcc-{^arch}\" } fn build(): String { \"built with {compiler()}\" } native = build() cross = build() with { arch = \"aarch64\" } build never mentions arch, yet cross is built with gcc-aarch64: the with reaches every call made inside it. Declaring a context # context NAME: TYPE = DEFAULT declares a context and its default, at the top level of a file. Every file of the workspace can read it without an import, so a name is declared only once. A context can leave out its default, context toolchains: List<Toolchain>; reading it then needs a with that sets it, and reading it without one is an error. A default is an expression, and it is evaluated where the context is read, in the context of the read. So one context can default to another, and follow it: Copytype Platform = {arch: String, os: String} context host_platform: Platform = {arch = \"x86_64\"; os = \"linux\"} context platform: Platform = ^host_platform target = ^platform.arch with { host_platform = {arch = \"aarch64\"; os = \"linux\"} } A default must be pure: it can't call an impure function. To use a value that needs one, compute it first and bind it with with. Setting a context with with # EXPR with { NAME = VALUE } evaluates EXPR with the context NAME set to VALUE. It applies to the call or field access just before it, so a + f() with {...} sets the context for f() alone. It must be on the line where the expression ends. To set a context for several statements, use the block form, with { NAME = VALUE } { ... }. A dotted name sets one field of a record context and keeps the others: Copytype Platform = {arch: String, os: String} context platform: Platform = {arch = \"x86_64\"; os = \"linux\"} fn triple(): String { \"{^platform.arch}-{^platform.os}\" } riscv = triple() with { platform.arch = \"riscv64\" } with { arch } is short for with { arch = arch }, setting the context from a variable of the same name. Reading a context in a parameter # A parameter written ^NAME takes its default from the context of that name, so a caller can still pass a value explicitly. fn f(^arch) is the same as fn f(arch: String = ^arch), with the type taken from the context's declaration: Copycontext arch: String = \"x86_64\" fn compiler(^arch): String { \"gcc-{arch}\" } usual = compiler() explicit = compiler(arch = \"mips\") Context and deferred work # A lazy keeps the context it was made in. Wherever it is later awaited, it reads the values that were set where it was written. A top-level field is computed when its file runs, so it reads the default values. Memoised functions stay correct: a memoised call's result is reused only for a call made in a context that gives every context the call read the same value.","word_count":550,"filters":{},"meta":{"crumbs":"GCSL / The Language","title":"Context"},"anchors":[{"element":"h2","id":"declaring-a-context","text":"Declaring a context #","location":107},{"element":"h2","id":"setting-a-context-with-with","text":"Setting a context with with #","location":266},{"element":"h2","id":"reading-a-context-in-a-parameter","text":"Reading a context in a parameter #","location":410},{"element":"h2","id":"context-and-deferred-work","text":"Context and deferred work #","location":477}]}},"word_data":{"memois":[{"position":522,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":527,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"avail":[{"position":27,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"result":[{"position":529,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"copycontext":[{"position":58,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":458,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"host_platform":[{"position":212,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":225,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":231,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"workspac":[{"position":133,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"linux":[{"position":220,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":238,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":371,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"everyth":[{"position":52,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"an":[{"position":138,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":172,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":177,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":248,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"then":[{"position":159,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"out":[{"position":151,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"os":[{"position":209,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":218,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":236,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":360,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":369,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":376,"weight":6,"original_word":null}],"as":[{"position":444,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"you":[{"position":18,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":37,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"riscv":[{"position":378,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":385,"weight":12,"original_word":null}],"matter":[{"position":3,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"some":[{"position":1,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"be":[{"position":243,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":313,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"run":[{"position":515,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"without":[{"position":8,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":137,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":169,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"list":[{"position":156,"weight":12,"original_word":null}],"platform":[{"position":17,"weight":24,"original_w